Google has put a new "world model" into the hands of paying subscribers, and it is easy to over-read. The official pages describe something narrower: a research-style tool that generates interactive worlds from a prompt. This post explains what Genie 4 is, who can use it today, how Google says you steer it and which questions Google has not answered yet.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. The product is made by Google, which competes with Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test the product and has no affiliate relationship with Google at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Genie 4 is

Project Genie is Google's browser-based experience for generating and exploring interactive worlds. Google says it first appeared earlier this year as an experimental research prototype. Genie 4 is the newest model behind it, which Google calls "our most realistic and interactive world model yet."

In plain terms, a video model produces a clip you watch, while a world model produces a scene you move through. According to the Google DeepMind page, you navigate and control the camera with keyboard controls and steer with text prompts. Google also calls Genie 4 its first real-time world model to generate audio natively, meaning music, ambient sound and dialogue are generated along with the visuals.

## What you can do in a session

Google lists a few specific abilities. You can type a new prompt in the middle of a session to change the weather, add a character or redirect the action. Gemini models adapt those prompts to the scene. The DeepMind page also says Genie 4 simulates scenarios such as aerodynamics and collisions, and that characters move and respond to their environment. It describes Gemini 3.8 Flash as a guiding agent that breaks a goal into steps and checks progress.

The launch is more than one model. The 18 November post says the Project Genie package includes a Project Genie agent that builds worlds through conversation, Gemini watching the session and suggesting where to go or triggering events, and a community gallery where people can publish, explore and remix worlds. Genie 3 stays in the model picker. Google says Genie 3 sessions now run up to 3 minutes per generation, up from 60 seconds, with options to link worlds and to seed a world from a 360-degree panorama image.

Notice what is missing: the pages we read do not give a session length for Genie 4 itself. Treat any specific session length you see elsewhere as unconfirmed.

## Who can use it today

Google says Genie 4 is available to "eligible Google AI Ultra subscribers" through Project Genie. Google AI Pro members can join a waitlist on the same page. For developers, the October announcement says early access partners will get it through the Gemini API and Google AI Studio, and we found no later date for that.

![Step chart of Genie 4 access: trusted testers from 27 October 2026, eligible Google AI Ultra subscribers from 18 November 2026, a Google AI Pro waitlist with no date and developer early access with no date](/images/site1/genie-4-project-genie-explained-who-can-use-it-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

The access path matters if you are deciding whether to care. The model was announced on 27 October 2026 as a research preview for trusted testers. Subscriber access followed on 18 November. The pages we read do not state a price for Genie 4 on its own, which countries are included, an age limit, daily usage caps or what "eligible" requires beyond holding an Ultra plan. If you are thinking of upgrading a plan for this, check Google's current plan page and the Project Genie page first, because those details can change.

## Tips from Google's prompt guide

Google DeepMind published a prompting guide, and its advice is practical. For the starting prompt, name the environment and its surfaces, choose a style from photorealistic to painterly to claymation, state the viewpoint, and describe the foreground and midground. You can check framing in an image preview, or upload your own image, and uploaded images work best when they are high quality, clear and well lit.

For characters, the guide says dialogue works best with one character in the scene. For the camera, say first person or third person in the prompt. During a session, prompt one clear change at a time, and add "No music" if you want only ambient sound.

## Limits and safety, as Google states them

Google is open about the weak spots. The DeepMind page calls world simulation "an early, evolving area of research" and says visual details and object permanence can drift over time, especially in longer sessions or after several edits. The 18 November post lists camera latency, complex physical interactions, real-time audio precision and long-term scene permanence as ongoing challenges.

On safety, Google says all video and audio generated in Project Genie carries SynthID watermarking and that model inputs and outputs go through safety screening. The October page also says Genie 4 includes strict safety guardrails and that internal safety evaluations ran before the preview. These are Google's statements, and we have no independent evaluation of them.

## What this means for a small business

For most small businesses, probably not much yet. Genie 4 is gated behind the Google AI Ultra plan, has no stated commercial terms on the pages we read and produces explorable worlds rather than finished video or marketing assets. Google names filmmaking, journalism, gaming and manufacturing as intended uses, plus training environments for robots and agents, but those are aims, not shipped workflows.

A sensible approach is to watch two things: whether Google AI Pro access opens (a waitlist exists today), and whether the developer access through the Gemini API gets a date and published terms. Until then, this is a technology to understand rather than a tool to plan around. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.
